<!-- v3 (2026-11-17, help overhaul): John's text, converted to the shared
     blocks in ui/css/help.css, brought up to date with the v2 screen, and
     edited to a Grade 8 reading level (editorial review, approved). -->
<p>MusicBrainz can be a goldmine of information, but it can also be a messy pool of data. MusicBrainz IDs matches your files to the official track list, so every detail is right.</p>

<h3>What it does:</h3>
<p>MusicBrainz often lists many versions of the same album, so a person MUST make one early decision: <strong><em>which</em></strong> album is this?</p>
<p>MusicBrainz IDs searches MusicBrainz so you can confirm which album you have. Then it writes these values into every file:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Artist ID (mb_artist_id)</li>
    <li>Album ID (mb_album_id)</li>
    <li>Album Release Group (mb_release_group_id)</li>
    <li>Country of Release (mb_release_country)</li>
</ul>
<p>It also writes each track's own MusicBrainz IDs and track number. Then it renames each file to MusicBrainz's title.</p>

<section class="steps">
    <h3>How to use this tool:</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Check the <strong>Local Source</strong>, <strong>Album</strong> and <strong>Artist</strong> fields. <strong>Tracks</strong> shows how many files are in the folder, which helps you find the right version.
            <p class="note">If you came from Unpack &amp; Convert, these are filled in for you.</p>
        </li>
        <li>Choose <span class="ui-button">Search MusicBrainz</span> (or press Enter). The matching releases appear below.
            <p class="note">If MusicBrainz doesn't have your album, you can add it at <a href="https://musicbrainz.org/release/add">musicbrainz.org</a>.</p>
            <p class="note">MetaForge shows up to 50 releases, best matches first. Releases with the same number of tracks as your folder go to the top. A release that isn't an official one says so under its format, for example <em>Bootleg</em> or <em>Promotion</em>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>Choose the row that best matches your album. Its details appear on the right, with <strong>Your files</strong> beside the <strong>MusicBrainz tracks</strong>.</li>
        <li>Check the two lists line up. If a file is in the wrong place, type its new position in <strong>Order</strong> and choose <span class="ui-button">Apply order</span>.</li>
        <li>When everything looks right, choose <span class="ui-button">Write IDs and rename files</span>.</li>
        <li>When it's done, choose <span class="ui-button">Continue to Intelli-Tagger</span>.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<h3>Why are we doing this?</h3>
<p>One of MetaForge's main jobs is to give every file high-quality tags. Writing these values into your files "aligns" your album with the global database. That's the main benefit of MusicBrainz today.</p>
<p>These values also help the next step, the <strong class="tool">Intelli-Tagger</strong>. It collects each track's original release date and the people who played on it. With that, the <strong class="tool">Intelligent Playlist Maker</strong> can build thoughtful, creative sets, not just random ones. Our motto: "Garbage in, garbage out. Quality in, quality out."</p>

<h3>A note about accuracy</h3>
<p>MusicBrainz is built by music fans around the world. Because it's <em>crowdsourced</em>, how accurate and detailed it is varies from album to album. MetaForge works hard to keep your tags accurate, but it can only use what MusicBrainz has.</p>

<section class="faq" aria-labelledby="mbid-faq">
    <h3 id="mbid-faq">Questions</h3>

    <details>
        <summary>What if none of the releases match my files?</summary>
        <p>First check the <strong>Album</strong> and <strong>Artist</strong> fields. The search uses exactly what's typed there, so try fewer words or a different spelling, then search again.</p>
        <p>If MusicBrainz still doesn't have your version, you can add it at <a href="https://musicbrainz.org/release/add">musicbrainz.org</a>, then search again here.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary>Is it OK to choose a bootleg?</summary>
        <p>Yes, if that's the version you have. Choose the release that matches your files: the number of tracks and their order matter most.</p>
        <p>The release's own year is written as the album's release year. The <strong class="tool">Intelli-Tagger</strong> finds each track's original year separately. So a 2006 bootleg of a 1978 album still gets 1978 as the original year.</p>
    </details>
</section>

<p>See also: <button type="button" class="see-also" data-mf-help="musicbrainz_submit" data-mf-help-name="MusicBrainz Submit">MusicBrainz Submit</button>, for giving credits back to MusicBrainz.</p>
